data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies the genomes of living organisms, based on a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Cas9 is a DNA-cutting enzyme. Delivered into a cell with a synthetic guide RNA, it cuts the genome at a chosen location [1][3].\n- Cas9 makes double-stranded DNA breaks, which the cell repairs in ways that allow gene inactivation, removal, or insertion [3].\n- In nature, CRISPR sequences come from bacteriophage DNA and help bacteria detect and destroy similar viral DNA in later infections [2].\n- CRISPR-Cas9 is widely used in genetic engineering, medicine, agriculture, and biotechnology [3]. A headline-only source reports a CRISPR breakthrough for treating diseases [4] and a cell-culture result on eliminating HIV [5]."}

